    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.4",
    "topic_title": "Carbohydrates",
    "stem": "A plant cell makes two different polysaccharides, and both are built entirely from glucose monomers joined by covalent bonds. The first is kept in granules and broken down when the plant needs energy. The second forms long, unbranched chains that pack tightly side by side within the cell wall. Which choice correctly identifies the second polysaccharide and its function?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Starch, which stores energy within the plant cell wall",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This reflects the misconception that carbohydrates serve only an energy role, so every plant glucose polymer must be starch. Starch is the plant's storage polysaccharide, which matches the first molecule in the granules, not the tightly packed chains in the wall. Fix: Ask whether the description points to storage or to support before you name the polysaccharide."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Glycogen, which stores energy in highly branched chains",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This confuses the storage polysaccharides of plants and animals. Glycogen is the branched glucose storage polymer of animals, kept in places such as liver and muscle. The stem also describes unbranched chains in a plant cell wall, which do not match glycogen. Fix: Check the organism and whether the chains are branched or linear before you match a polysaccharide."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Cellulose, which provides structural support for the plant cell wall",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Cellulose is a linear polymer of glucose. Its long, unbranched chains pack tightly together into strong fibers in the cell wall, so it supports the cell rather than serving as an energy reserve. This shows that a polymer's organization, not just its monomer, determines its function."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Glucose, which supplies readily usable energy to the cell",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "This mixes up the monomer with the polymer. Glucose is the monosaccharide building block, and as a free sugar it can be used quickly for energy. The stem asks about a polysaccharide built from many glucose units that forms long chains. Fix: Check whether the question describes a single monomer or a polymer of many joined monomers."
     }
    ]
   },
