    "subject_key": "biology",
    "unit_number": 1,
    "topic_code": "1.4",
    "topic_title": "Carbohydrates",
    "stem": "Starch and cellulose are both carbohydrates found in plants. Which statement correctly connects their structures to their biological functions?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "Starch and cellulose both contain glucose units, so both function primarily as energy-storage polysaccharides.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The same-monomer, same-function trap ignores how polymer organization affects function. Sharing glucose monomers does not make cellulose an energy-storage carbohydrate; its organization supports plant cell walls. Fix: Compare each polymer’s organization and biological role instead of assigning function from its monomer alone."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "Starch is a polysaccharide used for energy storage, whereas cellulose is a monosaccharide used for plant cell wall support.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The monomer–polymer confusion misclassifies cellulose. Cellulose consists of many covalently linked sugar monomers, making it a polysaccharide rather than a monosaccharide. Fix: Check whether each carbohydrate is a single sugar unit or a chain of sugar units before naming its type."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "Starch chains provide structural support in plant cell walls, whereas cellulose chains store glucose units for later energy use.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "The storage–support reversal assigns each polysaccharide the other’s role. Starch functions in energy storage, while cellulose contributes to plant cell wall support. Fix: Match each carbohydrate’s name to its biological role before connecting its polymer structure to that function."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "Starch is a polysaccharide that stores glucose units for later energy use, whereas cellulose is a polysaccharide whose chains are organized to support plant cell walls.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Both carbohydrates contain glucose monomers joined by covalent bonds, but their different organization supports different functions. Starch stores glucose units for later energy use, while cellulose chains form structural material in plant cell walls."
     }
    ]
